;; The `fallback` prop protects from implementation errors & user errors
;; when uiR is for some reason unable to compute width, height, or position
;; of a node. In that case we replace it with its source code representation.
;;
;; If not for the fallback prop, the UI would break in such cases; since e.g.
;; children of x-stack or y-stack ask their predecessor for w/h/l/t to compute
;; their own w/h/l/t. So the moment someone breaks in the chain, is the moment
;; the rest of the chain doesn't get rendered due to not knowing their l/t/w/h;
;; and all the user will see is nothingness beyond a certain point -- certainly
;; not good UX!

N←{¦ -final-w fallback: (¦ source_ rem_: (%number +i32))}
  <> {N: $'(d7uir →source rem: →rem code-only: true)}

N←{¦ -final-h fallback: (¦ source_ rem_: (%number +i32))}
  <> {N: $'(d7uir →source rem: →rem code-only: true)}

N←{¦ -dl fallback: (¦ source_ rem_: (%number +i32))}
  <> {N: $'(d7uir →source rem: →rem code-only: true)}

N←{¦ -dt fallback: (¦ source_ rem_: (%number +i32))}
  <> {N: $'(d7uir →source rem: →rem code-only: true)}
